\description{
Draws, for one or more KEGG pathways, the gene-gene relations parsed from
KEGG's KGML by \code{\link[=network_kegg_topology]{network_kegg_topology()}} (\code{PPrel} protein-protein,
\code{GErel} gene-expression, \code{ECrel} enzyme-enzyme): one box per gene, one
directed edge per relation. Boxes of genes the extract's compounds are
predicted to hit (\code{network_edges}) are filled by the highest probability
among those compounds and drawn larger the more compounds hit them;
other genes stay white. Edges follow KEGG's notation: colour and end
marker give the effect (arrow = activation / expression, flat bar =
inhibition / repression, no end = binding), line type the mechanism
(dashed = phosphorylation, dotted = indirect, ...).

When \code{pathway_id} is \code{NULL}, the \code{top_n_pathways} pathways with the most
target genes of the extract among their relations are drawn, one panel
each.
}
\section{Paralog boxes}{

A KGML box usually bundles several paralogs (e.g. \code{MAPK1}, \code{MAPK3}) that
take part in exactly the same relations; \code{\link[=network_kegg_topology]{network_kegg_topology()}}
expands such a box into one row per gene pair. With \code{collapse_paralogs = TRUE} (default) genes with identical incoming and outgoing relations are
merged back into one box, labelled \code{MAPK1/3} (hit genes listed first) --
close to how KEGG draws the pathway, and much less cluttered.
}

\section{Layout}{

\code{"sugiyama"} puts upstream genes on the left and downstream genes on the
right, in layers (Sugiyama et al. 1981; \code{\link[igraph:layout_with_sugiyama]{igraph::layout_with_sugiyama()}}),
which reads like a signalling cascade. \code{"auto"} (default) uses it for
every connected component whose relations are close to acyclic (a
feedback arc set of at most 20\% of its edges), and Fruchterman-Reingold
otherwise. Components are packed side by side, largest first.
}

\section{Full pathway vs. extract-only topology}{

\code{\link[=network_kegg_topology]{network_kegg_topology()}} keeps, by default (\code{restrict_to_network = TRUE}), only relations whose two genes are both targets of the extract --
every box is then filled. Run it with \code{restrict_to_network = FALSE} to
store the whole pathway, and the figure shows the extract's targets
within the full cascade (unfilled boxes = genes it does not hit).
}

